        // warp to avoid time checks
        vm.warp(1);
        // initialize the contract (needed for getRoyalties not to revert)
        // We need to call initialize on C. It expects (credChainId, credId, verificationType, protocolFeeDestination).
        // Provide dummy values that pass validation (protocolFeeDestination != address(0)).
        vm.prank(address(0xdead)); // initialize will set owner to msg.sender, but we just need to call it
        // Foundry's default test contract is address(this), so we need to prank as any address
        // The initialize function requires being called via proxy pattern (initializer modifier).
        // Since we deployed with new C() directly (not a proxy), the code length may cause issues.
        // To avoid setup complexity, we can instead test the property by deploying a minimal test that simulates the scenario.
        // However, the hard rules require using new C() as given.  The contract uses initializer which checks construction.
        // To make it work, we will call initialize directly (which may revert if called in a strange context).
        // Let's assume initialization succeeds by calling it from an EOA (this contract).
        // We'll use vm.prank to be the caller.
        // First, deal ETH to this test contract for potential fees.
        vm.deal(address(this), 1 ether);
        // Call initialize (the contract is not an upgradeable proxy, so initializer should work)
        c.initialize(1, 1, "test", address(0x1234));
        // Now call getRoyalties with the fuzzed tokenId
        // We need to ensure it does not revert (NotInitialized should not happen now)
        // The invariant: the default royaltyRecipient is never address(1).
